course: space
lesson: 22
title: Distances and light years
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Space is so big that kilometres stop being useful, so astronomers measure it by how far light travels, the fastest thing there is.
explain: A light year sounds like a time, but it is a distance: how far light goes in one year, about 9.5 trillion kilometres.
explain: Light is fast but not instant: sunlight that warms your face on the beach left the Sun about 8 minutes 20 seconds ago.
explain: So when you look at a star you see old light, like getting a postcard posted long ago: you see the star as it was, not as it is now.
explain: Shrink the Sun to a football in the middle of a pitch, and Earth is a peppercorn 25 metres away, while the next star is another football thousands of kilometres off.
fact: A light year is a distance, not a time: it is how far light travels in a year, about 9.5 trillion kilometres.
fact: Light from the Moon takes about 1.3 seconds to reach us, and radio signals travel just as fast, so a call to astronauts on the Moon has a short delay, and sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds.
fact: Astronomers measure the solar system in astronomical units, where 1 AU is Earth's average distance from the Sun, about 150 million km.
fact: The nearest star after the Sun is Proxima Centauri, about 4.2 light years away, or about 40 trillion km.
fact: Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is the farthest human-made object, and it is due to reach about one light-day from Earth, about 26 billion km, around late 2026.
fact: Even at Voyager's speed, a journey to Proxima Centauri would take about 75,000 years.
fact: When you look at a star, you see light that left it long ago, so you see the star as it was, not as it is now.
fact: If the Sun were a football in the middle of a pitch, Earth would be a peppercorn about 25 metres away, and the nearest star another football thousands of kilometres away.
fact: Professional astronomers often use the parsec, about 3.26 light years, a unit based on how stars seem to shift as Earth orbits the Sun.
fact: Bright stars are at very different distances: Sirius is about 8.6 light years away, Vega about 25, Polaris about 430, and Deneb probably more than 1,500.
fact: The Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 centimetres a year, about as fast as fingernails grow.
exercise: What does a light year measure? | A distance | A length of time | A speed | A brightness
exercise: About how far does light travel in one year? | About 9.5 trillion kilometres | About 150 million kilometres | About 9.5 million kilometres | About 365 kilometres
exercise: How long does sunlight take to reach Earth? | About 8 minutes 20 seconds | No time at all | About 1.3 seconds | About a whole day
exercise: How long does light from the Moon take to reach us? | About 1.3 seconds | About 8 minutes | About an hour | It arrives instantly
exercise: What is one astronomical unit? | Earth's average distance from the Sun | The distance light travels in a year | The width of the solar system | The distance to the Moon
exercise: Which star is nearest to us after the Sun? | Proxima Centauri | Sirius | Polaris | Vega
exercise: About how far away is Proxima Centauri? | About 4.2 light years | About 4.2 light minutes | About 420 light years | About 42 million km
exercise: What is the farthest human-made object? | Voyager 1 | The Hubble Space Telescope | The International Space Station | Perseverance on Mars
exercise: How long would a trip to Proxima Centauri take at Voyager's speed? | About 75,000 years | About 75 years | About 7,500 years | About 4 years
exercise: Why do we see a star as it was long ago? | Its light left it long ago | Stars are pictures of the past painted on the sky | Our eyes are slow to notice stars | Stars move backwards in time
exercise: In the football model, how far is the peppercorn Earth from the football Sun? | About 25 metres | About 25 centimetres | Thousands of kilometres | About 25 kilometres
exercise: About how many light years are in one parsec? | About 3.26 | About 1,000 | Exactly 1 | About 365
exercise: Which bright star is closest to us? | Sirius | Vega | Polaris | Deneb
exercise: About how far away is Polaris? | About 430 light years | About 4.3 light years | About 43,000 light years | About 8 light minutes
exercise: How fast is the Moon moving away from Earth? | About 3.8 centimetres a year | About 3.8 kilometres a year | About 1 metre a day | It is not moving away at all
exercise: Why do astronomers use light years instead of kilometres for stars? | The numbers in kilometres are far too huge | Kilometres do not work in space | Light years are smaller and more exact than kilometres | Stars are measured in time, not distance
test: Zoe says "a light year is a really long time". What's wrong? | A light year is a distance, not a time | It is actually quite a short time, about a month | It is a speed, how fast light goes, not a time | Nothing, it is just a year of time measured in space
test: If the Sun suddenly went out, how soon would we notice from Earth? | After about 8 minutes 20 seconds | At that very same moment | After about 1.3 seconds | After a whole year
test: Polaris is about 430 light years away. If it had blown up 100 years ago, what would we see tonight? | Polaris still shining as normal | A dark gap where Polaris was | Polaris exploding right now | Polaris getting brighter every night
test: bro says we'll fly to the nearest star in like 10 years with Voyager. lol? | No, at Voyager's speed it would take about 75,000 years | Yes, Voyager will get there in 2036 if it keeps going straight | Yes, it is only 4 light minutes away | No, it would take about 750 years
test: Which is true about bright stars in the sky? | They are at very different distances from us | They are all the same distance away from the Earth | The brightest ones are always the nearest ones to us | They all sit together on the inside of one big sphere
test: Talking to astronauts on the Moon, why is there a short delay before they answer? | Radio signals take about 1.3 seconds each way | The Moon's thin air slows the sound of their voices | Their radios are very old | The astronauts think slowly in low gravity
test: In a film, a ship flies "100 light years to the next star in two minutes". Why is it not real? | Nothing we know can travel faster than light | Stars are much closer than that, so the trip is too short | Light years are only used for measuring time, not distance | Ships cannot fly in the dark between stars without sunlight
test: Deneb looks about as bright as Vega. What does that tell us? | Deneb must give out far more light, as it is much farther away | Deneb and Vega must be exactly the same distance away from the Earth | Deneb is nearer but smaller | Deneb is a planet, not a star
test: Will the Moon fly off into space next year then, if it's moving away? | No, it moves away only about 3.8 centimetres a year | Yes, it moves away about a kilometre a day, faster and faster | Yes, it will leave us within ten years and drift off into space | No, it is actually getting closer to the Earth every single year
test: Why is the football model of the solar system hard to fit in a school field? | Even the nearest star would be thousands of kilometres off | The Sun football would be too heavy | The peppercorn would be far too big to fit on the field at all | The planets would all touch each other
test: How far is 1 AU in kilometres? | About 150 million | About 9.5 trillion | About 384,000 | About 26 billion
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the unit designated the light year? | It is a unit of distance equal to about 9.5 trillion kilometres | It is a unit of time equal to one Earth year, used for star travel | It is a unit of speed equal to that of light | It is a unit of brightness for stars
exam: Ascertain the approximate interval required for light emitted by the Sun to reach the Earth. | About eight minutes and twenty seconds | About one and three-tenths seconds | About twenty-four hours | About four years and two months
exam: Identify the star, other than the Sun, situated nearest to the Earth. | Proxima Centauri | Sirius | Polaris | Betelgeuse
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the observation of a distant star. | The light observed departed from the star long ago | The light observed departs from the star at that instant | The star is invariably observed as it will be in future | The light of stars requires no time to travel
